{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC2 rewritten + gate-level freshness assertion implemented (2026-08-28)\n\n**AC2 was REWRITTEN, not implemented.** The original wording demanded a write that cannot\nreach the shared store \"FAIL LOUDLY to the caller\", the exact opposite of standing operator\ndecision B4 on `e1ed-b700-c442-4d0c`. The scope change is recorded INLINE in the criterion\n(original wording quoted, B4 named as superseding, dated and marked operator-approved) so a\nfuture reader sees a decision rather than a criterion that always read that way.\n\n## What shipped\n\nA new READ-side module `src/rebar/_store/freshness.py`. Three gate paths refuse before\nconsulting an attestation, each reporting the distinct verdict `stale-store`:\n\n1. `llm/plan_review/attest_gate.claim_gate_check` — the claim gate. Chosen because it is the\n   single verdict function behind `claim`, `transition open in_progress` AND\n   `plan_review_status`, so one insertion covers the write gate and its read-only sibling\n   with no new call sites. Placed BEFORE `signing.verify_signature`, since on a stale store\n   that read is precisely what returns the wrong answer.\n2. `_commands/gates.close_plan_review_gate_check` — the plan-review close gate. Same harm,\n   same placement.\n3. `_commands/close_precheck._completion_precheck` — the completion close gate, immediately\n   after the gate-state/force resolution so a stale store never spends a billable LLM run.\n\n`signing.verify_signature` was deliberately EXCLUDED. It authorizes nothing; it reports.\nRefusing there would remove the very tool used to diagnose a stale store, and annotating its\nresult would churn a schema-pinned MCP read contract for information the gates already carry\nin their refusal. Read-side visibility instead comes through `plan_review_status`, which\nwraps `claim_gate_check` and therefore now reports `stale-store` for free.\n\n## Staleness definition (local, no network)\n\nA gate must not acquire a dependency on the remote being reachable, and every signal is\nalready on disk: the durable `rebar-push-pending` marker (`push-pending`), or HEAD\nbehind/diverged from the ALREADY-FETCHED remote-tracking ref (`behind` / `diverged`). Note\n`fsck` deliberately stays silent on *merely behind* because a writer ff-adopts on its next\npush — a gate is a pure reader and never triggers that adoption, so for certification it is\nthe sharpest available signal.\n\n## Refuse vs warn\n\nREFUSE. A gate that cannot trust its input has no business certifying with it. The refusal\nnames the condition with its count and gives the recovery, including the local-CLI fallback\n(the point being that the surface which went stale is a shared MCP server the caller cannot\nrestart). The probe itself fails OPEN — a broken diagnostic must not convince a healthy\nstore it is broken — while a PROVEN stale verdict fails CLOSED.\n\n## B4 is not violated\n\nThe change is confined to gate READ paths. `push_tickets_branch` still ALWAYS returns None\nand never raises, pinned by\n`test_a_stale_store_does_not_make_an_ordinary_write_raise`. Blanket write-refusal stays\nrejected: it would have write-blocked every session during the four-hour starvation window.\n\n## Verification\n\nRED-first, mutation-checked: 7/7 mutants killed (each production edit reverted individually;\nthe paired test failed every time). The decisive tests stub a CERTIFIED, VALID attestation\nand still require a refusal on a stale store, so only the freshness assertion can produce it.\nGates green: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, module-size contract, complexity ratchet\n(`new=0 increased=0`). The starvation fixes remain OUT of scope and escalated."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787954444139217001,"uuid":"8604752b-64c8-4be0-82a9-7d8c73ea3608"}